# Line-ending policy for a repo that is edited on Windows and executed inside a
# Linux container over the same bind mount, so both platforms read these exact
# files. Git for Windows' installer default is core.autocrlf=true; without the
# rules below that default commits CRLF, and a CRLF scripts/entrypoint.dev.sh
# fails at container start with "bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M".
#
# This file is committed, so it applies to every clone, every teammate and CI.
#
# File modes are handled separately and do not need a setting here: tracked
# files are 644 except scripts/*.sh at 755. The Windows drive is bind-mounted
# into the container over 9p/DrvFs, which reports arbitrary exec bits, so this
# checkout also carries core.filemode=false in .git/config to ignore them.
# That is a per-checkout convenience only — the committed modes above are what
# every other clone sees, so do not rely on it being set anywhere else.

# === Default ===
# Every tracked file here is text (the corpus and dish images under data/ are
# gitignored). Store LF in the repo, check out LF in every working tree.
# `text=auto` still runs Git's binary detection, so a binary added later is
# left untouched rather than mangled.
* text=auto eol=lf

# === Explicit LF — these are consumed by the container's shell and toolchain ===
*.sh         text eol=lf
*.py         text eol=lf
*.yml        text eol=lf
*.yaml       text eol=lf
*.toml       text eol=lf
*.json       text eol=lf
Dockerfile*  text eol=lf
.env.example text eol=lf
